# Build trees. The build is out-of-source -- `cmake -B build` and friends -- so
# an in-source CMakeCache.txt is a mistake worth seeing rather than ignoring.
/build/
/build-*/
CMakeUserPresets.json

# Reference papers. refs/Refs.md is the tracked index -- it says what each paper
# is and where to fetch it from -- but the PDFs themselves are publisher
# material and several MB each, so they stay out of the repository.
refs/*.pdf

# WSL writes an alternate-data-stream sidecar next to anything downloaded through
# Windows, recording the download zone. They arrive alongside the PDFs and are
# not matched by the rule above, since the name is "X.pdf:Zone.Identifier".
*:Zone.Identifier

# Editor and tooling droppings. compile_commands.json is generated by the build
# (CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS) and .cache/ is clangd's index of it.
compile_commands.json
.cache/
.clangd
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
tags

# LaTeX. docs/manual/ is the pre-Sphinx manual, built by its own Makefile from
# the .tex sources; everything latex and bibtex leave behind is regenerated,
# including the PDF. These patterns moved from docs/ to docs/manual/ when the
# Sphinx tree took over docs/ -- a pattern left at docs/*.aux would have matched
# nothing, and the by-products would have shown up as untracked.
docs/manual/*.aux
docs/manual/*.bbl
docs/manual/*.blg
docs/manual/*.fdb_latexmk
docs/manual/*.fls
docs/manual/*.idx
docs/manual/*.ind
docs/manual/*.log
docs/manual/*.nav
docs/manual/*.out
docs/manual/*.pdf
docs/manual/*.snm
docs/manual/*.synctex.gz
docs/manual/*.toc

# Sphinx. docs/ is the Read the Docs source tree; _build/ is entirely generated,
# and doctrees/ is Sphinx's incremental cache.
docs/_build/
docs/doctrees/

# MFEM run output. A solve writes its mesh and its solution as a .mesh/.gf pair,
# and VisIt/ParaView dumps as .vtk/.vtu. These patterns only ever affect files
# git does not already know about, so a new one that is meant to be committed
# will need `git add -f`. ( The exception this note used to describe, the
# tracked sample data under attic/free-boundary/vacuum-testing/, went with
# attic/ on 2026-09-07. )
*.mesh
*.gf
# THE ONE FIXTURE PAIR THAT IS SOURCE AND NOT OUTPUT. examples/limited-tokamak
# is a free-boundary machine case, and on it the initial guess is part of the
# PROBLEM STATEMENT rather than an optimisation -- a cold start does not
# converge at all. The guess is the reference equilibrium reconstructed by
# Green's functions over this domain, written by
# tools/freegs4e-benchmark/mkexactguess.py, and it cannot be regenerated at test
# time because the reference .npz it is built from is itself ignored (below) and
# needs freegs4e, scipy and a Picard solve to recreate.
!examples/limited-tokamak-guess.mesh
!examples/limited-tokamak-guess.gf
# AND THE SEVEN BENCHMARK MACHINES' GUESSES, BOTH CONDUCTOR MODELS, for the
# same reason and one more. They are part of the problem statement -- a cold
# start on a free-boundary machine case wanders and does not converge -- and
# make_all.sh writes them beside the TOML that names them, so the two are one
# artefact and only one of them was surviving a commit.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS COSTS IS FAR FROM ITS CAUSE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WORTH THE
# COMMENT. The filament set is tracked because somebody `add -f`'d it; the
# shaped set, generated later, was written by the generator, swallowed here,
# and never committed -- and the symptom was `race.py --shaped` aborting eight
# rungs later with an MFEM "Mesh file not found", naming a file the generator
# reports writing. A generated input that .gitignore eats does not fail at
# generation.
!examples/machine-*-guess.mesh
!examples/machine-*-guess.gf
# A MESH THAT IS A CONFIGURATION'S OWN BUILD PRODUCT, AND THE STAMP THAT SAYS
# WHICH GEOMETRY MADE IT. `[mesh.generate]` names a generator and meq-run runs
# it, writing the .msh the file's [mesh] File points at and a <mesh>.meq-mesh
# beside it holding the exact command. Both are regenerated from the TOML in a
# second, so neither is source -- while the .msh files that have no such block
# ARE source, since nothing in the tree records how to remake them.
*.meq-mesh
examples/diverted-tokamak-generated.msh
examples/limited-tokamak-filament.msh
examples/limited-tokamak-filament-curve.msh
# The freegs4e benchmark's seven machine cases, whose meshes tools/freegs4e-
# benchmark/make_all.sh regenerates from the references along with everything
# else in them.
examples/machine-*.msh
*.vtk
*.vtu
*.pvtu
# ParaViewDataCollection writes a DIRECTORY named after the run, holding a .pvd
# index and Cycle000000/ of pieces. The pieces are covered above; the .pvd was
# not, and without it git showed the whole directory as untracked -- a directory
# whose every entry is ignored is itself hidden, so this one line is what keeps
# a driver test from leaving `?? soloviev-nstx/` behind. The names come from
# user configs, so there is nothing more specific to match on.
*.pvd
# The driver's (R, Z) grid file. Same reasoning: run output, not source.
*.nc
# Configs the DRIVER TESTS write, to drive a variant of a shipped example
# without shipping a second one -- a prescribed contact against a found one, a
# degree against a degree. Run output rather than source, on the same reasoning
# as the .pvd line above: without this a DriverAcceptance run leaves half a
# dozen `?? driver-acceptance-*.toml` behind and a clean tree stops meaning
# anything.
driver-acceptance-*.toml
# And MachineFixedBoundary's, which is a DIRECTORY rather than a handful of
# files: it overrides [output] Directory on the shipped examples/fixed-*.toml
# so that running the test does not scatter six machines' worth of mesh, .gf,
# .vtu and .nc across the repository root. Anchored, per the note below.
/machine-fixed-boundary-scratch/
visit_dump*/
ParaView/

# Build products from the compiler and the profilers.
*.o
*.d
*.a
*.so
gmon.out
*.gcda
*.gcno
*.gcov

# Scratch directories at the root. Anchored with a leading slash deliberately:
# an unanchored pattern matches at every depth, so `/runs/` here cannot quietly
# swallow some future tests/runs/ as well. `git check-ignore -v <path>` names
# the line responsible when something has gone missing.
/scratch/
/runs/
/tmp/

__pycache__/
.venv/

# THE BENCHMARK VENVS, AND THEY ARE A GIT ACCIDENT WAITING TO HAPPEN.
# tools/freegs4e-benchmark/venv has always been untracked and has always relied
# on nobody running `git add -A`; tools/desc-benchmark/venv is **1 GB** of JAX
# and makes that reliance expensive. Each is reproducible from its directory's
# README and neither belongs in history.
tools/*/venv/

# The `docs` target's virtualenv, holding Sphinx and its theme. It is a
# separate environment because docs/requirements.txt pins Sphinx below 8 for
# the theme, and nothing else should be held to a docs constraint.
.venv-docs/
docs/manual/*.rubbercache

# The freegs4e benchmark's REFERENCE data. fgsref.py writes one <case>.npz of
# grids and profile arrays per configuration, a <case>.json of the scalars, and
# a summary.json across a scan -- all of it regenerated by rerunning the script,
# and the 513^2 runs are megabytes each. The scripts are tracked and the output
# is not, which is the same line drawn for every other run product here.
tools/freegs4e-benchmark/*.npz
tools/freegs4e-benchmark/*.json
# And the same, one directory down: baseline.py writes each rung of the nested
# ladder into its own ref-n<N>/, which the two patterns above do not reach.
tools/freegs4e-benchmark/ref-n*/

# Claude Code's own working area at the repository root. It holds `worktrees/`,
# and each entry there is a real GIT WORKTREE of THIS repository -- a directory
# with its own `.git` file pointing back into `.git/worktrees/`. So `git add -A`
# does not merely stage some scratch: it warns about adding an embedded
# repository and, taken at face value, commits a gitlink to a checkout that
# exists only on this machine. Anchored, like the scratch directories above.
/.claude/
